# Every environment variable this system needs, and — the part that has bitten us
# twice — which runtime owns each one.
#
# There are three places variables get set, and they are NOT interchangeable:
#
#   ## Next.js-side       `.env.local` locally, `vercel env add` on Vercel.
#   ## Convex-side        `npx convex env set NAME value` (add `--prod` for production).
#   ## Vercel build only  set in the Vercel dashboard; belongs in no file.
#
# A variable set in the wrong runtime is silently inert. It produces no error, no
# warning, and the fix looks applied:
#
#   2026-08-02  INTERNAL_API_SECRET was set on Vercel but never on production
#               Convex. Every quotes.create threw «No autorizado» and the chatbot
#               told every Customer their request could not be registered.
#   2026-08-05  An expired IMAP_PASSWORD was replaced on Vercel, where nothing
#               reads it. The mailbox poller is a Convex action. It stayed down.
#
# So: copy this file to `.env.local` for the Next.js-side values, and run the
# `npx convex env set` lines for the Convex-side ones. Doing only the first gives
# you an app that starts and a pipeline that does not run.
#
# Convex-side variables are per-deployment. Production's live on
# `colorless-chinchilla-754`; a preview deployment gets the Convex project's
# *default* environment variables. See `docs/deployment.md` before changing those
# defaults — IMAP_* in particular must stay out of them.
#
# `src/lib/env-example.test.ts` fails if this file and the source disagree.


## Next.js-side

# Copy to `.env.local`. On Vercel these are set per-environment with `vercel env add`.

# --- Auth (Clerk) ---
# Read by the Clerk SDK, not by our code. Unset, the app does not start: the SDK
# throws while building the middleware, before any route runs.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY is inlined at build time, so changing it on
# Vercel does nothing until a build runs.
# Production currently runs on a Clerk *development* instance (pk_test_/sk_test_)
# because Clerk will not issue a production instance for a *.vercel.app domain —
# docs/deployment.md, ticket 27.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# In-app paths, and the values below are the real ones. They do not change
# between Clerk instances. Unset, Clerk falls back to its hosted pages instead of
# the sign-in screens in this app — a working app, but not this app's.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_URL=/sign-in
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_UP_URL=/sign-up

# --- Convex, as seen from the frontend ---
# Which Convex deployment `npx convex dev` talks to. The CLI writes this itself on
# first run, so copy this file to .env.local BEFORE running `npx convex dev` —
# copying afterwards replaces the real value with the placeholder below.
# Local only; never set it on Vercel, where CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY chooses the target.
# Unset, the CLI asks which deployment you mean instead of failing.
CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT=dev:your-deployment-name

# The Convex client's URL. Unset it crashes the app: `ConvexReactClient` gets a
# non-null assertion in src/components/providers/ConvexClientProvider.tsx.
# On Vercel it is overwritten at build time by `convex deploy`, so the stored
# value is only a fallback (docs/deployment.md, "Which deployment a build targets").
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL=https://your-deployment-name.convex.cloud

# The `.site` origin for Convex HTTP Actions, which is where Next reaches Convex's
# internal functions. Optional and deliberately unset on Vercel:
# src/lib/internal-api.ts derives it from NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL by swapping
# `.convex.cloud` for `.convex.site`, so it follows whichever deployment the build
# targeted. Setting it explicitly pins every preview at production's boundary.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_SITE_URL=https://your-deployment-name.convex.site

# --- Public base URL ---
# Used by src/app/api/send-rejection-email/route.ts for the links in the rejection
# email. Unset, those links are omitted rather than pointed at localhost. The
# Quote Document logo no longer depends on it (ticket 17): it is embedded as a
# data URI by src/lib/quote-logo.ts.
# Its Convex-side twin is APP_URL below — same idea, two runtimes, two variables.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Mail ---
# Every Resend call lives in a Next.js route handler. Nothing under `convex/` reads
# this, so a copy on the Convex deployment is inert — remove it if you find one.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Where the Approver notification is sent. Unset, `to:` is undefined and the send
# fails. Read on BOTH sides: Next uses it as the recipient (chat and poller-alert
# routes), Convex uses it as the fallback Approver allowlist when APPROVER_EMAILS
# is unset (convex/lib/approvers.ts). Set it in both places, to the same address.
ADMIN_EMAIL=ventas@example.com

# The address of the mailbox the system polls. Read on BOTH sides, for different
# reasons: Convex logs into it (below), and Next sets it as the `replyTo` on the
# Approver's emails so their reply comes back to a mailbox that is being read —
# without it the reply goes to the sending address and nothing ever polls it.
# It is the one IMAP_* variable that is not Convex-side only.
IMAP_USER=cotizaciones@example.com

# --- Machine-to-machine ---
# The shared secret between Next and Convex's internal HTTP boundary. Required on
# BOTH sides and the two values must be identical. This is the 2026-08-02 outage:
# a real string compared against `undefined` denies everything, and it reads as an
# authorisation bug rather than a missing variable. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
INTERNAL_API_SECRET=

# --- Model ---
# Required on BOTH sides. Next calls the model in the chat route; Convex calls it
# too, because convex/emails.ts imports src/lib/gemini-parser.ts to read the
# Approver's reply. Unset on either side, that half stops working.
GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=


## Convex-side

# Set with `npx convex env set NAME value`, and again with `--prod` for production.
# Putting any of these in `.env.local` does nothing: Convex functions read the
# Convex deployment's environment, not this file.

# Where Convex reaches back into Next to trigger the Quote Document, the Approver
# reply routes and the poller alert. Unset, `webhookBaseUrl()` warns and every
# webhook is silently skipped — the pipeline stops after the Approver replies.
# In local development this points at your dev server; Convex Cloud cannot reach a
# localhost, and that now fails as a logged fetch error rather than silently.
# APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Auth (Clerk), the Convex half ---
# convex/auth.config.ts reads `CLERK_JWT_ISSUER_DOMAIN || ''`, so a missing or
# stale value does not throw: getUserIdentity() returns null and every signed-in
# Customer looks signed out.
# CLERK_JWT_ISSUER_DOMAIN=https://your-instance.clerk.accounts.dev

# Verifies the Clerk webhook that populates `users` (convex/http.ts). Also present
# on Vercel, where it does nothing — the only reader is a Convex action.
# CLERK_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- The inbound mailbox ---
# Read by convex/emails.ts, a Convex action, on the five-minute cron. Apart from
# IMAP_USER (see the Next.js-side section — it is genuinely both), the only
# Next.js reader is /api/debug-imap, which returns 404 in production. That is why
# replacing these on Vercel changes nothing (2026-08-05).
# Leave them OUT of the Convex project's default environment variables, or every
# open branch polls the one shared Gmail inbox and races production for the
# Approver's replies. HOST, USER and PASSWORD all unset is a deliberate, silent
# no-op — that is the mechanism keeping previews off the mailbox.
# IMAP_PASSWORD is a Google app password, not the account password.
# IMAP_HOST=imap.gmail.com
# IMAP_PORT=993
# IMAP_PASSWORD=

# Also required Convex-side, as the mailbox login. Same value as the Next.js-side
# line above; they must agree, or replies land somewhere nothing polls.
# IMAP_USER=cotizaciones@example.com

# --- Who may approve ---
# The Approver allowlist, comma-separated. Authority to move a Replacement Request
# comes from this list and nothing else; the REQ- code travels in every email
# subject, so knowing one cannot be enough. Unset it falls back to ADMIN_EMAIL
# (with a warning), and with neither set the list is empty and authorises nobody —
# replies go unread rather than the mailbox standing open.
# APPROVER_EMAILS=ventas@example.com,otro@example.com

# Also required Convex-side, as the APPROVER_EMAILS fallback. Same value as above.
# ADMIN_EMAIL=ventas@example.com

# Also required Convex-side. Must be byte-identical to the Next.js-side value.
# INTERNAL_API_SECRET=

# Also required Convex-side: convex/emails.ts interprets the Approver's reply.
# GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=


## Vercel build only

# Set in the Vercel dashboard, on Preview and Production. Not a file value — it is
# a credential the build uses, and it belongs in neither `.env.local` nor here.
# Generate both under Convex dashboard → Settings → Deploy keys.
#
# It must be a DIFFERENT KIND of key per environment: a production deploy key on
# Production, a *preview* deploy key on Preview. A production key on Preview would
# let any open pull request push schema to production. See docs/deployment.md.
#
# Unset, `npm run build` fails and nothing ships.
# CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY=
